    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p8-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Expand $(a+b+c+1)^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a^2+b^2+c^2+2ab+2ac+2bc+2a+2b+2c+1$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(a+b+c+1)^2&=(a+b+c)^2+2(a+b+c)+1\\\\(a+b+c+1)^2&=a^2+b^2+c^2+2ab+2ac+2bc+2a+2b+2c+1\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Let $a$, $b$ and $c$ be positive integers for which $a^2+b^2+c^2+ab+ac+bc=20$. Find the value of $a+b+c$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No such positive integers exist.\n\nThe possible sums are at most $5$, and none of their positive-integer triples satisfies the equation.",
          "worked": "Let $s=a+b+c$ and $Q=a^2+b^2+c^2+ab+ac+bc$. Expanding the square gives\n$$\\begin{align*}s^2&=a^2+b^2+c^2+2ab+2ac+2bc\\\\2Q&=s^2+a^2+b^2+c^2.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $a$, $b$ and $c$ are positive integers, $a^2\\geq a$, $b^2\\geq b$ and $c^2\\geq c$. Therefore, with $Q=20$,\n$$s^2+s\\leq40.$$\nAlso $s\\geq3$. If $s\\geq6$, then $s^2+s\\geq42$, so $s$ must be $3$, $4$ or $5$.\n\nUp to rearrangement, the possible triples and their values of $Q$ are:\n\n$(1,1,1)$: $Q=6$;\n\n$(1,1,2)$: $Q=11$;\n\n$(1,1,3)$: $Q=18$;\n\n$(1,2,2)$: $Q=17$.\n\nNone gives $Q=20$, so there is no value of $a+b+c$ satisfying the printed conditions.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 116
      }
    ]
  },
